 # GPU Splat Rasterization via Vertex/Fragment Shaders
 
-Also called: Point Primitive Splatting, Shader-based Splatting
+Also called: Point Primitive Splatting, Shader-based Splatting, Splat Rendering via OpenGL ES 2.0
 
 ## Overview
-This method implements splat rasterization on GPUs by simulating a dedicated rasterization unit using standard graphics pipeline stages: vertex shaders for transformation and bounding box calculation, point primitives for fragment generation, and fragment shaders for kernel evaluation and depth computation [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently projecting 3D splats onto the screen while maintaining accurate depth values and filtering weights, optimizing for minimal instruction count in the fragment shader [S1]. It is more efficient than previous GPU splatting implementations because the fragment shader requires only three computational instructions (two MADs and one texture fetch), whereas previous approaches were more expensive [S1].
+This method implements splat rasterization on GPUs by simulating a dedicated rasterization unit using standard graphics pipeline stages: vertex shaders for transformation and bounding box calculation, point primitives for fragment generation, and fragment shaders for kernel evaluation and depth computation [S2]. It solves the problem of efficiently projecting 3D splats onto the screen while maintaining accurate depth values and filtering weights, optimizing for minimal instruction count in the fragment shader [S2]. It is more efficient than previous GPU splatting implementations because the fragment shader requires only three computational instructions (two MADs and one texture fetch), whereas previous approaches were more expensive [S2].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-Splats are sent to the GPU using the standard point primitive [S1]. The output is a rasterized image with accumulated attributes and depth values, where each fragment corresponds to a pixel covered by a splat [S1].
+Splats are sent to the GPU using the standard point primitive [S2]. The output is a rasterized image with accumulated attributes and depth values, where each fragment corresponds to a pixel covered by a splat [S2].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-Each splat $k$ is represented by its inverse mapping matrix $\mathbf{N}_k$ and its center position [S1]. A 2D texture stores the precomputed 2D reconstruction kernel weights $r_0(x)$ [S1].
+Each textured splat carries 15 floating-point values: center (3 floats), major axis (3 floats), minor axis (3 floats), texture coordinates of center (2 floats), and texture coordinates of endpoints of both axes (4 floats) [S1]. The 2D reconstruction kernel is precomputed in a 2D texture [S2].
 
 **Steps:**
-1. The vertex shader computes the inverse mapping matrix $\mathbf{N}_k$ for each splat [S1].
-2. The vertex shader computes a conservative 2D axis-aligned bounding box of the projected splat [S1].
-3. The position of the point primitive is set to the center of this bounding box [S1].
-4. The size of the point primitive is set to the largest dimension of the bounding box [S1].
-5. The fragment shader receives the matrix $\mathbf{N}_k$ transmitted as three column vectors [S1].
-6. The fragment shader performs the matrix multiplication to transform the fragment's screen-space coordinates into the splat's local space using only two MAD (Multiply-Add) instructions [S1].
-7. The weight $r_0(x)$ is retrieved from the 2D kernel texture via a single projective texture fetch [S1].
-8. The accurate window-space depth value is extracted from the fourth component of the matrix product result [S1].
-9. Fragments outside the splat are discarded using the alpha test unit [S1].
-10. Attributes are multiplied by the weight and accumulated using the blending unit [S1].
